\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The cops didn\'92t take Sarah and Chanta to a hospital. They took them to one each.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 They chauffeured Sarah to a palace in the sky, where she would have access to the best care, including positive affirmation of her Airborne birthright. Chanta, they rolled out of the cruiser onto a crumbling tenement-clinic rooftop below the Layer and left him there. Alexander agreed it would have to do.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The flowers, fruit baskets, muffin hampers, lingerie, plush menageries of teddy bears, bunny rabbits, unicorns, and sex toys in Sarah\'92s medical suite were refreshed daily. The cards were always signed identically:\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f2\i\fs22 Best wishes and get well soon\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>I\'92m glad to have saved your life\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'97Alexander Shakti
\f1\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Who was this loon? Sarah wondered. It took her no time at all to identify the celebrity host of Candid Criminal. In a fit of disgust, she dumped the day\'92s vibrators in with the muffins. The teddy bears and bunny rabbits quaked with fright. She cursed Alexander\'92s name. There was only one man who had really saved her from the bombing: Mr Fairy Boxers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah didn\'92t need any of the advanced procedures on offer. She recovered from her injuries in just two days. Instead of investigating her miraculous recovery, the doctors, nurses, bathroom attendants, and professorial boffins all chalked it up to superior Airborne genes, even speculating aloud that eugenics might\'92ve been abandoned a little too soon.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta regained consciousness, ignored and untreated, in a sweaty bed amid a maze of bacterially alarming smudges. He grimaced at the decay of the big ward: the greys were too green and yellow, the greens and yellows too brown, the browns too much like the vomit he coughed up once his skull started to hurt. Nurses gave him dusty pills the size of party pies and told him to rest.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Overcome by the pungent scents of Alexander\'92s flower baskets, Sarah checked herself out early. She felt fully recovered anyway and was anxious to thank Chanta.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 When she climbed the fire-escape stairs and entered Chanta\'92s ward, she almost gagged from the stench. However, she was determined to press on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94Can I help you?\'94 the orderly at the ward desk asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I doubt it.\'94 Sarah strode past the orderly with authority. Among the endless rows of paint-chipped bed-frames, saggy mattresses and saggy patients, she almost passed Chanta without recognition.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sarah!\'94 he called out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She gasped when he held her gaze with his unblinking right eye. His blackened left eye was bruised shut and seeped a dilute blood-ooze that trickled over the lumps, scratches, and bruises that contrived something not too dissimilar to a face.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, my tax accountants! What are they doing to you?\'94 She fled to his side and grasped his hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ow!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She released his hand. \'93Are they even treating you?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The doctors say it\'92s acute sunburn.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'85 your eyebrows and most of your hair are singed off! And the cuts and bruises?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Shaving accident, they said.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You shave your\'85 knuckles?\'94 Sarah held Chanta\'92s hands. \'93This is appalling!\'94 she sputtered. \'93I\'92ve never seen such\'85 squalor\'85\'94 
\f2\i Only a quick hover-copt jump from where I live
\f1\i0 , she thought, but could not bring herself to admit it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve never seen Cloudborne healthcare before, then? All they prescribe is sticking plaster and suppositories.\'94 Chanta was about to remark that he had a better medical kit at home, but thought wiser of making such facts known in public spaces.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah straightened her spine with decision-making. \'93You\'92re coming with me,\'94 she said. \'93He\'92s coming with me,\'94 she announced to the ward.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You can do better, dearie,\'94 said a withered nurse stumbling by on a cane.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94I need a wheelchair,\'94 Sarah demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Thank you, that would be most kind,\'94 Chanta said to Sarah.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The withered nurse cackled.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Sarah returned a half-hour later, her fingers tingling with the excitement of her thievery, having purloined a wheelchair from an Airborne hospital. Chanta sat in it, fortified with admirable resolve and tenacity. Sarah wheeled him to the stairwell, where they met two orderlies she paid to carry Chanta-in-wheelchair to the ground floor. From there, she pushed and shoved across unreliable ground to the nearest hover-pad.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 They made most of the journey to Chanta\'92s apartment discreetly by Cloudborne ground cab, which served to enhance Sarah\'92s experience and understanding of the deteriorating Cloudborne lifestyle. Everything she saw was worn-down, broken, taped, jury-rigged, bent to shape, chipped, corroded, smeared, or tied up with string. \'93These are a few of my ghastliest things,\'94 Sarah sang under her breath. The seats of the ground cab were torn and exposed springs. The doors to the back seat were clearly from another model of vehicle entirely, and didn\'92t shut properly. Gaps between the doors and wheel arches allowed Sarah to see through to the street. The steering wheel was almost completely missing. The driver steered by grasping the stubs of the steering wheel spokes. She braked by stomping on the floor like she was exterminating cockroaches. She accelerated by rocking back and forward in her seat, as if that would quicken the tired vehicle\'92s pace.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When they reached Chanta\'92s front door, he presented his armpit, but his door stayed resolutely shut.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It didn\'92t even read me,\'94 he complained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You might have to stand up,\'94 Sarah said. She helped him to his feet and supported him while he lifted his arm again. The door clicked open, and Chanta slumped into the wheelchair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not exactly disability-friendly,\'94 Chanta said solemnly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No,\'94 Sarah agreed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was dismayed by the size and condition of Chanta\'92s living space, but did her best to hide it. Chanta wheeled himself to his room, bundled onto the bed, and passed out.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Over the next few days, Sarah curtailed her overtime work\'97the office culture was creepier than any she\'92d encountered in many a year\'97and took every opportunity to work from home, all of which gave her more spare time to devote to Chanta\'92s recovery. She risked the trip to his apartment twice a day so she could feed, clothe, and bathe him (boxer shorts resolutely stayed on), and tend to his wounds with Airborne bandages, creams, unguents, and toenail clippers. She filled his stasis unit with microwave meals. She bought him new socks, cologne, and vodka he could actually drink. She subscribed him to the best on-demand Xtreme Fighting Porn, and he marvelled at the tag-team doggy hammer throws. Although scars remained encamped on his face, hands and chest, his health and demeanour improved.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 One night, Sarah was folding Chanta\'92s laundry as he sat gingerly on the couch, his skin still taut and tender, and watched some streams.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I don\'92t know why you don\'92t throw these out, Mr Fairy Boxers,\'94 Sarah said as she held up his threadbare underwear.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I like them,\'94 Chanta said defensively.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ll get you some new ones.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta scoffed. \'93I got those in Floormart. An Airborne would never be caught dead in a Floormart.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged. \'93I\'92ll send someone then. My cleaner does odd jobs for me; he\'92ll do it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Then 
\f2\i he
\f1\i0  might be caught dead there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s turn to scoff: \'93That\'92s urban myth; no one\'92s ever died in a stock delivery accident.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Despite his discomfort, Chanta sat up straight and leant forward. \'93Have you ever seen a Floormart?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s a supermarket; how bad can it be?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do you even know why they call it Floormart?\'94 Sarah shook her head, trying to make it a no, but only managed a non-committal shrug and a guilty smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There are no shelves, that\'92s why. All the merch is just dumped on the floor.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, I get it: it\'92s low-rent. But that doesn\'92t mean people die in them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta had forgotten his burns. \'93Floormarts are just open basement pits with tin roofs. Trucks back up to the edge and tip their loads straight down into the store. No walls, no barriers\'97they can dump anywhere: kitchen goods over there, clothing over here, food wherever. Everything just tumbles down from street level and piles up on the basement concrete. It\'92s a shopper's problem to dodge the avalanche when deliveries arrive. I was once digging through the dry food section looking for fusilli and found a body at the bottom of a pasta mountain. Whatever you do, don't go near a Floormart.\'94
\f0\fs24 <!$Scr_Ps::1>}